<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salabat Jung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1718">1718</a>–<a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salabat Jung was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Asaf Jah I</a> who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> in 1751 with the support of the French under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-de-bussy">Marquis de Bussy</a>. Dependent on French troops, he assigned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-circars">Northern Circars</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1753, and his army was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> at Udgir in 1760. He was deposed by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-ali-khan">Nizam Ali Khan</a> in 1762 and killed in prison in 1763.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salabat-jung.jpg" alt="Salabat Jung" width="195" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salabat_Jung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Salabat Jung was born Mir Sa'id Muhammad Khan Siddiqi Bayafandi on 24 November 1718, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Asaf Jah I</a>. During the Second Carnatic War he was a prisoner, first in the camp of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-jung">Nasir Jung</a> and then in that of his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-jung">Muzaffar Jung</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> killed Muzaffar Jung on 13 February 1751, the French under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-de-bussy">Marquis de Bussy</a> proclaimed him Nizam near the Lakkireddipalli Pass, and the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamgir-ii">Alamgir II</a> later gave him the title Amir-ul-Mamalik.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He kept the French in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, restored their rights and privileges and gave de Bussy the title Saif-ud-daula Umdat-ul-Mulk. In March 1751 he granted them villages including Nizampatnam, Kondavid, Narsapur and Yanaon. Soon after he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna River</a>, 25,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> under the Peshwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaji-baji-rao">Balaji Baji Rao</a>, allied with his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> ud-Din Khan Feroze Jung II, confronted him, but the Peshwa withdrew when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damaji-rao-gaekwad">Damaji Rao Gaekwad</a> invaded the Deccan from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>.</p><p>Salabat Jung then entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> in great pomp and rewarded his allies generously. Supplied through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machilipatnam">Machilipatnam</a>, de Bussy raised his European troops to 500 and his native soldiers to 5,000. Within a month, in May 1751, Salabat Jung left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a> to meet the threat from the north. His army grew mutinous for want of pay, and in April 1752 Afghan soldiers assassinated Raja Raghunath Das; Haider Jung of Machilipatnam, trusted by de Bussy, became his new councillor.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-circars">Northern Circars</a> he ceded to the French gave them 470 miles of coast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel Coast</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machilipatnam">Machilipatnam</a>'s dyeing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> industry, the teak forests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajahmundry">Rajahmundry</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> of Srikakulam. The Khilwath Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> was built under his direction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-ali-khan">Nizam Ali Khan</a>, Asaf Jah II, deposed him on 8 July 1762 and had him held in the prison of Bidar Fort, where he was killed on 16 September 1763. He was buried at Bidar, outside the dargah of Multani Pasha.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salabat Jung” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Asaf Jah’s death in 1748 his sons and grandson fought over the succession with French and British backing, and <b>Salabat Jung</b> owed his throne to the French commander the Marquis de Bussy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.36,78.48&amp;year=1747" aria-label="Hyderabad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6682 1519 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Salabat Jung"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7132 1789h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7132 1789h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.36,78.48&amp;year=1747">What was here in 1747? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
